#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308205 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ac953-c511-5d6f-902d-4aeb804c99ce" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff7fcd72-7b6a-5156-a3c9-4a1862e148ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5+3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308207} {:answer/id #uuid "85e026bc-5a06-57fe-a39d-4415f3110507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308208} {:answer/id #uuid "43cad395-6f95-5566-b226-feaea161f645" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308209} {:answer/id #uuid "c5dd3b3f-18c7-52ce-8a75-3b6277acdb3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308210} {:answer/id #uuid "d92fd167-4e5b-5f69-bf4e-f278d70e519c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43cad395-6f95-5566-b226-feaea161f645" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308209} :answer-field/id #uuid "8136771c-6f38-55d7-8461-51b6d50d5644" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9dd1eb6a-d2e0-570d-98d8-cddfe7919baf" :question/math-academy-id "q-97193" :question/problem "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨a,\\,(4-\\text{i})b+c⟩$ if  \n\n$\\,$ $⟨b,a⟩=-1-\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,c⟩=5.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is antilinear in the second argument:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,\\,(4-\\text{i})b+c⟩ & =⟨a,(4-\\text{i})b⟩+⟨a,c⟩ \\\\  & =(\\overset{―}{4-\\text{i}})⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩ \\\\  & =(4+\\text{i})⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4+\\text{i})⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c\\text{ }⟩ & =(4+\\text{i})\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}+⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨b,a⟩$ and $⟨a,c⟩:$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4+\\text{i})\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}+⟨a,c⟩ & =(4+\\text{i})\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-1-\\text{i}})+5 \\\\  & =(4+\\text{i})\\cdot (-1+\\text{i})+5 \\\\  & =-5+3\\text{i}+5 \\\\  & =3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
